{{smaller block|For-why he bosteth and ''braggeth'' with many bolde othes.
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (B), xiii. 281.}}}}

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|<poem>Conceit, more rich in matter than in words,
''Brags of'' his substance, not ''of'' ornament.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', R. and J., ii. 6.}}}}
{{smaller block|It was ''bragged'' by several Papists that upon such a day, or in such a time, we should find the hottest weather that ever was in England; and words of plainer sense.
{{right|''Pepys'', Diary, III. 3.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Yet, lo! in me what authors have to ''brag on!''
Reduced at last to hiss in my own dragon.</poem>
{{right|''Pope'', Dunciad, iii. 285.}}}}
'''2†.''' To sound, as a trumpet; blare; bray.
{{smaller block|Whanne the voyce of the trompe . . . in ȝoure eeris ''braggith.''
{{right|''Wyclif'', Josh. vi. 5 (Oxf.).}}}}
'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To boast of. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|He ''brags'' his service.
{{right|''Shak.'', Cymbeline, v. 3.}}}}
{{smaller block|Bear thy good luck with you when you cross these paved stones, and by our Lady, you may ''brag'' Scotland.
{{right|''Scott'', Abbot, I. xvii.}}}}
'''2†.''' To blow (a trumpet).
{{smaller block|Thane the Bretones boldely ''braggene'' theire tromppez.
{{right|''Morte Arthure'', l. 1484.}}}}

'''brag''' (brag), ''n.'' [< ME. ''brag''; from the verb.] '''1.''' A boast or boasting; a vaunt; also, boastfulness.
{{smaller block|What owtward ''brag'' so euer is borne by them, is in deed, of it selfe, and in wise mens eyes, of no great estimation.
{{right|''Ascham'', The Scholemaster, p. 34.}}}}
{{smaller block|Life invests itself with inevitable conditions, which the unwise seek to dodge, which one and another brags that he does not know; brags that they do not touch him; but the ''brag'' is on his lips, the conditions are in his soul.
{{right|''Emerson.''}}}}
'''2.''' A thing to boast of; source of pride.
{{smaller block|Beauty is Nature’s ''brag.''
{{right|''Milton'', Comus, l. 745.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>The sprout of an aik,
Bonnie, and blooming, and straight was its make;</poem>}}

readings.c3s1:
¶
{{smaller block|Who ever saw true learning, wisdom, or wit, vouchsafe mansion in any proud, vain-glorious, and ''braggartly'' spirit?
{{right|''Chapman'', Iliad, iii., Comment.}}}}

'''braggartry''' (brag′ärt-ri), ''n.''; pl. ''braggartries'' (-riz). [< ''braggart'' + ''-ry''.] Vain boasting; boastfulness. ''Mrs. Gore.'' [Rare.]

'''braggat†''', ''n.'' Same as ''bragget''<sup>2</sup>.

'''bragger''' (brag′ėr), ''n.'' [< ME. ''braggere''; < ''brag'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] One who brags.
{{smaller block|Evere ware thes Bretons ''braggeres'' of olde.
{{right|''Morte Arthure'', l. 1348.}}
The loudest ''braggers'' of Jews and Grecians are found guilty of spiritual ignorance. {{right|''Hammond'', Sermons, p. 627.}}}}

'''bragget<sup>1</sup>†''', ''n.'' An obsolete form of ''bracket''<sup>1</sup>.

'''bragget<sup>2</sup>†''' (brag′et), ''n.'' [Also written ''braggat'', ''bracket'', ''braket'' (and, after W., ''bragaut'', ''bragaud'', ''bragawd'', ''bragoe''), formerly also ''brackwort'', Sc. ''bragwort'', ''bregwort'' (in simulation of ''wort''<sup>2</sup>); < ME. ''braget'', ''bragat'', ''bragot'', < W. ''bragawd'', ''bragod'', a kind of mead (= Corn. ''bregaud'', ''bragot'', a kind of mead, = Ir. ''bracat'', malt liquor), < ''brag'' (= Ir. ''braich'' = Gael. ''braich''), malt, < ''bragio'', issue, sprout, = Gael. ''brach'', ferment, = Ir. ''bracaim'', I ferment; perhaps akin to E. ''brew''<sup>1</sup>.] A kind of mead made of ale boiled with honey, seasoned with pepper, cloves, mace, cinnamon, nutmegs, and fermented with wort or yeast.
{{smaller block|His mouth was sweete as ''bragot'' is or meth.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Miller’s Tale, l. 75.}}
Good ale, perrys, ''bragoes'', syder, and metheglins, was the true auntient British and Troyan drinks.
{{right|''Marston'', Dutch Courtezan, v. 1.}}
<poem>And we have served there, armed all in ale,
With the brown bowl, and charged with ''braggat'' stale.</poem>
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Gypsies Metamorphosed.}}}}

